/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccst-networking.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccst-networking", [["cs1",0,1,"A web page request moves down the stack on a PC. At which TCP/IP layer are TCP port numbers added?",["Application","Transport","Internet","Network access"],1,"TCP and UDP port numbers belong to the Transport layer. The Internet layer adds IP addresses, not ports.","Objective 1.1",["The Application layer creates the request itself (HTTP, DNS); it does not add port numbers to a segment.",null,"The Internet layer adds source and destination IP addresses, not TCP or UDP ports.","The Network access layer adds MAC addresses and turns frames into signals; ports are already in place by then."],3],["cs2",0,1,"Which address does a switch use to forward a frame within the local network?",["The destination IP address","The destination port number","The destination MAC address","The default gateway address"],2,"Switches forward Ethernet frames by destination MAC address. IP addresses are used by routers to move packets between networks.","Objective 1.1",["Routers use destination IP addresses to move packets between networks; a Layer 2 switch looks only at the frame's MAC header.","Port numbers identify applications at the Transport layer, and a basic switch does not read them to forward frames.",null,"The default gateway address is what a host uses to reach other networks; a switch does not forward frames based on it."],3],["cs3",0,1,"What is the unit of data called at the data link layer?",["Frame","Segment","Packet","Bit"],0,"Layer 2 encapsulates data in frames. Packets are Layer 3 and segments are Layer 4.","Objective 1.1",[null,"A segment is the Layer 4 (transport) unit that carries TCP or UDP port numbers.","A packet is the Layer 3 unit that carries IP addresses, one layer above frames.","Bits are the Layer 1 physical-layer representation, not the data link unit."],1],["cs4",0,1,"A customer pays for a 1 Gbps internet plan, but a speed test over Wi-Fi shows 380 Mbps. What does the 380 Mbps figure describe?",["Bandwidth","Round-trip latency","Jitter","Throughput"],3,"Throughput is the data rate actually achieved. Bandwidth is the 1 Gbps maximum capacity of the plan.","Objective 1.2",["Bandwidth is the maximum capacity of the link, which here is the 1 Gbps the customer pays for.","Latency is a delay measured in milliseconds, not a data rate in Mbps.","Jitter is the variation in delay between packets, measured in milliseconds, not a transfer rate.",null],3],["cs5",0,1,"A technician wants to measure the real data rate between two office PCs without using the internet. Which tool fits best?",["iperf","A web speed test","nslookup","tracert"],0,"iperf sends test traffic between a client and server that you control, so it measures the local path. A web speed test measures the path to an internet server instead.","Objective 1.2",[null,"A web speed test measures the path to an internet server, which is exactly what the technician wants to avoid.","nslookup queries DNS to resolve names; it does not measure data rates.","tracert lists the router hops and their delays along a path; it does not measure throughput."],2],["cs6",0,1,"A user on a video call says voices arrive late but clear. Which measurement describes how long data takes to travel from sender to receiver?",["Bandwidth","Latency","Throughput","Packet size"],1,"Latency is the delay for data to cross the network. Bandwidth is capacity and does not by itself describe delay.","Objective 1.2",["Bandwidth is the capacity of the link; plenty of bandwidth can still arrive with a long delay.",null,"Throughput is the actual data rate achieved, which describes how much gets through, not how long it takes.","Packet size affects efficiency slightly but is not a measurement of travel time across the network."],2],["cs7",0,1,"A university connects the networks in its library, dorms and science buildings on one campus. What type of network is this?",["PAN","WAN","CAN","MAN"],2,"A campus area network links buildings on one site. A MAN covers a city, and a WAN spans larger distances.","Objective 1.3",["A PAN covers a few meters around one person, such as Bluetooth devices, not a whole campus.","A WAN spans long distances such as cities or countries, usually through a service provider, not buildings on one site.",null,"A MAN covers a metropolitan area such as a city, which is larger than a single campus."],2],["cs8",0,1,"Wireless earbuds are paired with a phone using Bluetooth. What type of network is this?",["PAN","WLAN","LAN","CAN"],0,"A personal area network covers a few meters around one person. A WLAN is Wi-Fi covering a building or home.","Objective 1.3",[null,"A WLAN is a Wi-Fi network covering a home or building, not a short-range Bluetooth link.","A LAN connects devices across a building or home, usually with Ethernet and switches, not a phone-to-earbud pairing.","A CAN links several buildings on one campus, far larger than a personal Bluetooth link."],1],["cs9",0,1,"A company links its offices in three different countries through a service provider. What type of network is this?",["MAN","LAN","CAN","WAN"],3,"A wide area network connects sites over long distances. A MAN is limited to about one city.","Objective 1.3",["A MAN covers about one city, not offices in three countries.","A LAN covers a single building or site, not links between countries.","A CAN connects buildings on one campus, not sites in different countries.",null],2],["cs10",0,1,"A small business wants email and documents without managing any servers or operating systems. Which cloud model fits?",["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],2,"With SaaS the provider runs the whole application. IaaS still requires the customer to manage operating systems.","Objective 1.4",["IaaS provides virtual servers, so the business would still install and manage operating systems.","PaaS still requires the customer to build and manage their own applications on the platform.",null,"On-premises means the business hosts and manages its own servers, the opposite of what it wants."],2],["cs11",0,1,"A company keeps its payroll servers in its own data center but runs its website on a public cloud provider. Which deployment is this?",["Hybrid cloud","Private cloud","Public cloud only","Community cloud"],0,"Combining on-premises or private resources with public cloud is a hybrid deployment. A private cloud alone would not include the public provider.","Objective 1.4",[null,"A private cloud is infrastructure dedicated to one organization; it would not include the public provider hosting the website.","Public cloud only would mean the payroll servers are also in the public cloud, but they stay in the company data center.","A community cloud is shared by several organizations with common needs, which does not describe this setup."],2],["cs12",0,1,"A developer rents virtual machines and installs and patches the operating system on each one. Which service model is being used?",["SaaS","IaaS","PaaS","FaaS"],1,"IaaS provides virtual servers, storage and networking, and the customer manages the OS. PaaS would hide the OS from the developer.","Objective 1.4",["With SaaS the provider runs the whole application, so the customer never installs or patches an OS.",null,"PaaS hides the operating system; the developer deploys code and the provider patches the OS.","FaaS (serverless) runs individual functions with no servers or OS for the customer to manage."],2],["cs13",0,1,"Which statement about TCP is correct?",["It sends data without any acknowledgments","It is the protocol DHCP relies on to lease out IP addresses","It has no way to detect lost data","It sets up a connection with a three-way handshake"],3,"TCP is connection-oriented: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, then acknowledged and retransmitted data. DHCP uses UDP, not TCP.","Objective 1.5",["TCP acknowledges received data; sending without acknowledgments describes UDP.","DHCP uses UDP ports 67 and 68, not TCP.","TCP uses sequence numbers and acknowledgments to detect lost data and retransmit it.",null],2],["cs14",0,1,"A firewall must allow secure web browsing. Which port should be open?",["TCP 443","TCP 8080","UDP 69","TCP 21"],0,"HTTPS uses TCP 443. TCP 8080 is a common alternate port for plain, unencrypted HTTP.","Objective 1.5",[null,"TCP 8080 is a common alternate port for plain HTTP or proxies, not the standard port for secure browsing.","UDP 69 is TFTP, a simple file transfer protocol with no web use.","TCP 21 is the FTP control channel, not HTTPS."],1],["cs15",0,1,"PCs on a network are not getting their time synchronized from the time server. Which protocol and port should be checked?",["SNMP on UDP 161","DNS on UDP 53","NTP on UDP 123","TFTP on UDP 69"],2,"Network Time Protocol uses UDP 123. DNS on 53 resolves names and does not set clocks.","Objective 1.5",["SNMP on UDP 161 is used to monitor and manage devices, not to synchronize clocks.","DNS on UDP 53 resolves names to IP addresses and does not set the time.",null,"TFTP on UDP 69 transfers files such as configs and images, not time data."],2],["cs16",0,1,"A technician needs to copy a switch configuration to a simple server that has no login or authentication. Which protocol is commonly used?",["SFTP","HTTPS","SSH","TFTP"],3,"TFTP (UDP 69) is a simple transfer protocol with no authentication, often used for device configs and images. SFTP requires a login.","Objective 1.5",["SFTP runs over SSH and requires a login, so it does not match a server with no authentication.","HTTPS is for encrypted web traffic and normally requires a web server with some login for uploads.","SSH is an encrypted remote-login protocol that requires authentication.",null],2],["cs17",0,1,"Which protocol does the ping command use?",["ICMP","UDP","ARP","DHCP"],0,"Ping sends ICMP echo requests and waits for echo replies. ARP resolves IP addresses to MAC addresses and is not what ping uses.","Objective 1.5",[null,"UDP is a transport protocol with ports; ping works at the Internet layer with ICMP echo messages, not UDP.","ARP maps IP addresses to MAC addresses on the local link; it may run before a ping but is not what ping sends.","DHCP assigns IP addresses to hosts and has nothing to do with testing reachability."],1],["cs18",0,1,"Which pair of protocols normally uses UDP rather than TCP?",["HTTP and HTTPS","DNS queries and DHCP","SSH and FTP","SMTP and HTTPS"],1,"Standard DNS queries (UDP 53) and DHCP (UDP 67/68) use UDP. SSH and FTP use TCP.","Objective 1.5",["HTTP and HTTPS normally run over TCP (80 and 443) because web pages need reliable delivery.",null,"SSH (22) and FTP (20/21) both use TCP for reliable sessions.","SMTP (25) and HTTPS (443) both use TCP."],2],["cs19",0,2,"Which of these addresses is a private IPv4 address?",["172.32.4.10","192.169.1.5","11.0.0.8","172.20.15.3"],3,"The private range 172.16.0.0/12 covers 172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255, so 172.20.15.3 is private. 172.32.4.10 is just outside that range.","Objective 2.1",["172.32.4.10 is just past the end of 172.16.0.0/12, which stops at 172.31.255.255, so it is public.","192.169.1.5 is outside 192.168.0.0/16; only 192.168.x.x is private.","11.0.0.8 is outside the 10.0.0.0/8 private range, so it is a public address.",null],3],["cs20",0,2,"Twenty devices in a home all reach the internet through one public IPv4 address. What makes this possible?",["PAT (NAT overload)","DNS","DHCP relay (IP helper)","VLAN tagging"],0,"Port address translation maps many private addresses to one public address using different port numbers. DHCP hands out addresses but does not translate them.","Objective 2.1",[null,"DNS translates names into IP addresses; it does not let many devices share one public address.","DHCP relay forwards DHCP requests to a server on another subnet; it does not translate addresses.","VLAN tagging separates traffic into virtual LANs on a switch and has nothing to do with sharing a public IP."],2],["cs21",0,2,"A laptop shows the address 169.254.40.12 and cannot reach anything. What is the most likely cause?",["The DNS server is down","The laptop has a duplicate IP address","The laptop did not get a reply from a DHCP server","The default gateway is configured with the wrong address"],2,"169.254.x.x is an APIPA address a device assigns itself when DHCP fails. A DNS problem would not change the IP address.","Objective 2.1",["A DNS outage breaks name resolution but does not cause the laptop to give itself a 169.254.x.x address.","A duplicate IP address causes conflicts, but a DHCP-assigned address would still be in the normal range, not APIPA.",null,"A wrong gateway stops traffic to other networks, but the laptop would still have its normal DHCP address."],3],["cs22",0,2,"Which subnet mask matches the prefix /26?",["255.255.255.0","255.255.255.192","255.255.255.128","255.255.255.224"],1,"/26 is 24 bits plus 2 more: 128 + 64 = 192. 255.255.255.224 is /27.","Objective 2.2",["255.255.255.0 is /24, two bits shorter than /26.",null,"255.255.255.128 is /25, one bit shorter than /26.","255.255.255.224 is /27, one bit longer than /26."],2],["cs23",0,2,"How many usable host addresses are in a /27 subnet?",["30","32","62","14"],0,"/27 leaves 5 host bits: 2^5 = 32, minus the network and broadcast addresses = 30. 32 is the total, not usable, count.","Objective 2.2",[null,"32 is the total number of addresses in a /27, including the network and broadcast addresses, which cannot be assigned.","62 usable hosts belongs to a /26, which has 6 host bits.","14 usable hosts belongs to a /28, which has 4 host bits."],3],["cs24",0,2,"A host is 10.1.1.70/26. What is its network address?",["10.1.1.0","10.1.1.32","10.1.1.64","10.1.1.70"],2,"/26 uses blocks of 64 (0, 64, 128, 192). 70 falls in 64–127, so the network is 10.1.1.64.","Objective 2.2",["10.1.1.0 is the network for hosts .0 to .63; .70 is in the next /26 block.","Blocks of 32 belong to a /27; a /26 uses blocks of 64, so .32 is not a /26 network boundary.",null,"10.1.1.70 is the host's own address, not the network address, which has all host bits set to 0."],3],["cs25",0,2,"PC A is 192.168.5.20/25 and PC B is 192.168.5.140/25 with no router between them. Why can't they ping each other?",["They are in different subnets","They share the same broadcast address","A /25 allows only two hosts","Both addresses are public"],0,"A /25 splits the network at .128: .0–.127 and .128–.255, so the PCs are in different subnets and need a router. They do not share a broadcast address.","Objective 2.2",[null,"They do not share a broadcast address: PC A's is .127 and PC B's is .255, which is part of why they are in different subnets.","A /25 has 7 host bits and allows 126 usable hosts; two hosts is a /30.","Both addresses are in 192.168.0.0/16, which is a private range."],3],["cs26",0,2,"What is the broadcast address of 192.168.8.0/24?",["192.168.8.0","192.168.8.1","192.168.255.255","192.168.8.255"],3,"The broadcast address has all host bits set to 1, which is .255 in a /24. 192.168.8.0 is the network address.","Objective 2.2",["192.168.8.0 is the network address, with all host bits set to 0.","192.168.8.1 is the first usable host address, often a gateway, not the broadcast.","192.168.255.255 would be the broadcast for 192.168.0.0/16, not for a /24.",null],3],["cs27",0,2,"Which is a valid shortened form of 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0010?",["2001:db8::1","2001:db8::10","2001:db8:0:10","2001::db8::10"],1,"Remove leading zeros and replace the zero run with :: once, giving 2001:db8::10. The :: can appear only once, so 2001::db8::10 is invalid.","Objective 2.3",["2001:db8::1 ends in 1, but the original last hextet is 0010, which shortens to 10, not 1.",null,"2001:db8:0:10 has only four hextets and no ::, so it does not represent the full eight-hextet address.","The :: shortcut can be used only once; two of them make the address ambiguous and invalid."],3],["cs28",0,2,"An IPv6 address begins with fe80. What type of address is it?",["Global unicast","Multicast","Link-local","Loopback"],2,"fe80::/10 is link-local, used only on the local link. Multicast addresses start with ff.","Objective 2.3",["Global unicast addresses are in 2000::/3 and usually start with 2 or 3, not fe80.","Multicast addresses start with ff, not fe80.",null,"The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, not an address starting with fe80."],2],["cs29",0,2,"A laptop builds its own IPv6 global address from the router's advertised prefix without a DHCP server. What is this called?",["SLAAC","APIPA","PAT","DHCPv4"],0,"Stateless address autoconfiguration uses the prefix from router advertisements. APIPA is an IPv4 fallback, not IPv6.","Objective 2.3",[null,"APIPA is the IPv4 169.254.x.x fallback when DHCP fails; it does not use router-advertised prefixes.","PAT translates many private addresses to one public address and does not assign addresses to hosts.","DHCPv4 is a server-based IPv4 protocol, while the laptop here builds an IPv6 address without a server."],2],["cs30",0,3,"A technician must run a network cable 70 meters across an office ceiling and support 10 Gbps. Which cable is the best choice?",["Cat 5e","Cat 6a","Cat 3","RG-6 coax"],1,"Cat 6a supports 10 Gbps up to 100 m. Cat 5e is rated for 1 Gbps.","Objective 3.1",["Cat 5e is rated for 1 Gbps; it is not specified for 10GBASE-T over 70 m.",null,"Cat 3 is old voice-grade cable that supports only about 10 Mbps Ethernet.","RG-6 coax is used for cable TV and cable modems, not for 10 Gbps Ethernet runs to office devices."],2],["cs31",0,3,"Which connector is used on a standard Ethernet patch cable?",["RJ-11","BNC","F-type","RJ-45"],3,"RJ-45 is the 8-pin connector used for twisted-pair Ethernet. RJ-11 is the smaller telephone connector.","Objective 3.1",["RJ-11 is the smaller 6-position connector used for telephone and DSL lines.","BNC is a bayonet coax connector used on old Ethernet and video gear, not modern patch cables.","F-type is a threaded coax connector for cable TV and cable modems.",null],1],["cs32",0,3,"Two buildings are 5 km apart. Which cable should connect them?",["Single-mode fiber","Multimode fiber","Cat 6 shielded copper cable","Coaxial cable"],0,"Single-mode fiber carries signals for many kilometers. Multimode is limited to much shorter runs, and copper Ethernet stops at 100 m.","Objective 3.1",[null,"Multimode fiber is designed for shorter runs, typically hundreds of meters, not 5 km.","Copper Ethernet, shielded or not, is limited to 100 m per run.","Coaxial cable is not used for Ethernet links of this length between buildings and would not reach 5 km at modern speeds."],2],["cs33",0,3,"A cable modem connects to the provider's wall outlet with a threaded, screw-on connector. Which connector is it?",["LC","RJ-45","F-type","ST"],2,"Cable TV and cable internet use coax with F-type screw-on connectors. LC and ST are fiber connectors.","Objective 3.1",["LC is a small latching fiber connector, not a threaded coax connector.","RJ-45 is a clip-in twisted-pair Ethernet connector, not a screw-on coax connector.",null,"ST is a round bayonet fiber connector, not used for cable TV coax."],2],["cs34",0,3,"A switch has an empty slot that accepts a hot-swappable module so it can connect to fiber. What is this module called?",["PoE injector","SFP transceiver","Patch panel","Media keystone"],1,"SFP (small form-factor pluggable) transceivers plug into switch slots to support fiber or copper. A patch panel is a passive termination point, not a module.","Objective 3.1",["A PoE injector adds power to an Ethernet cable; it is an external box, not a module that adds fiber support.",null,"A patch panel is a passive rack termination point for cable runs, not a pluggable switch module.","A keystone is a snap-in jack for wall plates or panels; it does not plug into a switch slot."],2],["cs35",0,3,"Which fiber connector is small, square and commonly found in pairs on SFP modules?",["BNC","RJ-11","F-type","LC"],3,"LC is the small form-factor fiber connector common on SFPs. BNC and F-type are coax connectors.","Objective 3.1",["BNC is a round bayonet coaxial connector, not a fiber connector.","RJ-11 is a copper telephone connector, not fiber.","F-type is a threaded coax connector for cable TV and modems.",null],1],["cs36",0,3,"Users near a break room lose 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi each time the microwave oven runs. What is the best fix?",["Move those users to the 5 GHz band","Lower the AP's channel to 1","Switch the network to WEP","Increase the DHCP lease time for those laptops"],0,"Microwave ovens interfere in the 2.4 GHz band; 5 GHz avoids that interference. Changing security settings does nothing for radio interference.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Channel 1 is still in the 2.4 GHz band, and microwave ovens interfere across much of that band, so the drops would continue.","WEP is a broken security setting and has no effect on radio interference.","DHCP lease time controls how long an address is kept; it cannot stop the radio signal being drowned out."],3],["cs37",0,3,"Which statement correctly compares cellular and Wi-Fi networks?",["Wi-Fi uses licensed spectrum and cellular does not","Both require an Ethernet cable to the device","Cellular uses licensed spectrum run by a carrier","Cellular is limited to a single building"],2,"Cellular (4G/5G) runs on spectrum licensed to carriers. Wi-Fi uses unlicensed bands, so the first option reverses them.","Objective 3.2",["This reverses the facts: Wi-Fi uses unlicensed bands and cellular uses licensed spectrum.","Neither needs an Ethernet cable to the device; both are wireless technologies.",null,"Cellular networks cover wide areas through carrier towers; it is Wi-Fi that is typically limited to a building."],3],["cs38",0,3,"Which three 2.4 GHz channels do not overlap in North America?",["1, 6 and 11","2, 7 and 12","1, 5 and 9","3, 8 and 13"],0,"Channels 1, 6 and 11 are the standard non-overlapping 2.4 GHz set in North America. Other combinations overlap.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Channels 2, 7 and 12 are 5 apart like 1/6/11 but are not the standard set, and channel 12 is not normally allowed for Wi-Fi in North America.","Channels 1, 5 and 9 are only 4 channels apart, so their 20 MHz signals overlap.","Channel 13 is not normally used in North America, and 3/8/13 is not the standard non-overlapping plan."],1],["cs39",0,3,"A visitor needs to join the office guest Wi-Fi using WPA2-Personal. What must they know?",["The AP's MAC address and serial number","The SSID and the passphrase","The switch VLAN ID and gateway","Their username and a smart card"],1,"WPA2-Personal requires the network name (SSID) and the pre-shared passphrase. Username-based logins are for WPA2-Enterprise.","Objective 3.2",["The AP's MAC address and serial number identify the hardware; a client does not need them to join.",null,"VLAN IDs and gateways are assigned by the network after joining; the visitor does not enter them.","A username and smart card are credentials for WPA2-Enterprise with 802.1X, not WPA2-Personal."],2],["cs40",0,3,"A copper cable run between a switch and a PC is 130 meters long and the link is slow with many errors. What is the problem?",["The cable is too short","The PC needs a static IP","The run exceeds the 100 m Ethernet limit","The switch needs an SFP module"],2,"Twisted-pair Ethernet is limited to 100 m; beyond that attenuation causes errors. The IP configuration has nothing to do with signal loss.","Objective 3.2",["The cable is too long, not too short; short cables are not a cause of errors on Ethernet.","Signal loss on a long cable is a physical-layer issue; a static IP would not change it.",null,"An SFP module is used for fiber or special copper uplinks; the existing copper run is simply too long."],3],["cs41",0,3,"A smart thermostat, a door sensor and a security camera are all on the network. What category of endpoints are these?",["Servers","Thin clients","Workstations","IoT devices"],3,"Internet of Things devices are single-purpose, connected sensors and appliances. Servers provide services to many clients.","Objective 3.3",["Servers provide services such as files or email to many clients, unlike single-purpose sensors.","Thin clients are low-power computers that run a remote desktop, not sensors and appliances.","Workstations are full user computers such as desktops, not single-purpose connected devices.",null],2],["cs42",0,3,"On a Windows PC, which command shows the IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers and MAC address?",["ipconfig /all","netstat -an","ping -t","tracert -d 8.8.8.8"],0,"ipconfig /all shows the full adapter configuration. Plain ipconfig leaves out DNS and MAC details, and netstat shows connections.","Objective 3.4",[null,"netstat -an lists active connections and listening ports, not the adapter's address settings.","ping -t pings a host continuously; it tests reachability and does not show configuration.","tracert -d traces the route to a host without name lookups; it does not display local adapter settings."],1],["cs43",0,3,"A Linux user wants to see the addresses assigned to each interface. Which modern command should they run?",["ipconfig /all","ip addr","show ip interface brief","nslookup"],1,"ip addr (ip a) lists interfaces and addresses on Linux. ipconfig is the Windows command.","Objective 3.4",["ipconfig /all is a Windows command and does not exist on Linux.",null,"show ip interface brief is a Cisco IOS command, not a Linux command.","nslookup queries DNS for name resolution; it does not list interface addresses."],1],["cs44",0,3,"An iPhone user says Wi-Fi shows connected but apps do not load. Where can the technician check the phone's IP address and router address?",["The App Store","The phone's Bluetooth settings","The Wi-Fi settings, info icon next to the network","The Battery settings"],2,"On iOS, tapping the info (i) icon beside the joined Wi-Fi network shows the IP address, subnet mask and router. Bluetooth settings do not show IP details.","Objective 3.4",["The App Store installs apps and has no network configuration details.","Bluetooth settings manage paired devices and do not show Wi-Fi IP settings.",null,"Battery settings show power usage, not network addresses."],2],["cs45",0,4,"An engineer asks what a switch port LED is doing, and the technician reports solid green that flickers when a PC is used. What does this usually mean?",["The port is disabled","The port has a link and is passing traffic","The port has failed POST","The port is blocked by Spanning Tree Protocol"],1,"Green means link up, and blinking green shows activity. Amber usually indicates blocked, disabled or faulty.","Objective 4.1",["A disabled port usually shows no light or amber, not green with activity.",null,"A failed POST shows up on the system LED, typically amber, rather than a green port with activity.","A port blocked by STP shows amber, not green."],2],["cs46",0,4,"A newly connected switch port stays amber for a long time, while other ports turn green. What should the technician report to the engineer?",["The port is working normally at full speed","The PoE budget has increased","The port may be blocked, disabled or faulty","The switch firmware is up to date"],2,"A steady amber port light on a Cisco switch commonly means the port is blocked (for example by STP), disabled or has a fault. Green would indicate normal link.","Objective 4.1",["A port working normally shows green, not a long-lasting amber.","Amber port lights do not indicate the PoE budget, and PoE budgets do not increase on their own.",null,"Port LEDs say nothing about firmware versions."],3],["cs47",0,4,"The system LED on a Cisco switch is off and none of the port lights are on. What should the technician check first?",["The DNS server","The VLAN configuration","The DHCP scope","The power supply and power cable"],3,"If the system light is off the switch likely has no power. VLAN and DHCP settings cannot affect the system LED.","Objective 4.1",["A DNS server problem cannot turn off the switch's system and port LEDs.","VLAN settings change which ports share a broadcast domain but do not turn off the LEDs.","A DHCP scope affects addresses handed to clients, not the switch's own lights.",null],2],["cs48",0,4,"An engineer sends a diagram showing SW1 Gi1/0/24 connecting to R1 G0/0. What should the technician do?",["Connect a patch cable from port Gi1/0/24 on SW1 to port G0/0 on R1","Connect any free switch port to the router's console port","Connect SW1 port 1 to R1 port 24","Connect R1's USB port to the first free SFP slot on SW1 using a console cable"],0,"Diagrams list the exact device and interface at each end of a link, so the cable goes from SW1 Gi1/0/24 to R1 G0/0. Connecting any free port would not match the design.","Objective 4.2",[null,"The console port is for management, and the diagram calls for a specific data link between named interfaces.","This swaps the port numbers; the diagram names Gi1/0/24 on SW1 and G0/0 on R1.","A console cable is for CLI management and cannot create a routed data link into an SFP slot."],2],["cs49",0,4,"After patching a cable from the diagram, the technician wants to confirm it is in the right port without logging in. What is the quickest check?",["Reboot the switch","Look for the link light on the expected port turning green","Replace the patch panel","Change the port's VLAN"],1,"The port LED shows link as soon as the other end is connected, confirming the physical connection. Rebooting disrupts users and proves nothing.","Objective 4.2",["Rebooting the switch disrupts every user and does not show which port the cable is in.",null,"Replacing the patch panel is a major change and does nothing to confirm the new connection.","Changing the VLAN is a configuration change that could break the port; it does not verify the physical patch."],2],["cs50",0,4,"Which port is used to manage a new Cisco router that has no IP address configured yet?",["An SFP port","A PoE port","The console port","A serial WAN port"],2,"The console port gives out-of-band CLI access with no IP needed. Serial ports connect to WAN links, not management terminals.","Objective 4.3",["An SFP port is a data port for fiber or copper modules and needs IP configuration to be used for management.","A PoE port is a data port that also supplies power; it is not an out-of-band management port.",null,"A serial port connects to WAN circuits, not to a technician's terminal."],1],["cs51",0,4,"A VoIP phone has no power adapter but lights up once plugged into the switch. What feature is the switch port providing?",["Power over Ethernet","Port security","Link aggregation","VLAN trunking"],0,"PoE delivers power over the Ethernet cable to phones, APs and cameras. Trunking carries VLANs but no power.","Objective 4.3",[null,"Port security limits which MAC addresses can use a port; it supplies no power.","Link aggregation bundles several links for more bandwidth; it does not power devices.","Trunking carries multiple VLANs over one link but does not deliver power."],1],["cs52",0,4,"Which cable connects a laptop's USB port to the RJ-45 console port on a Cisco switch?",["A Cat 6 crossover cable","A coax jumper","A single-mode fiber patch","A USB-to-RJ-45 console (rollover) cable"],3,"Console access uses a rollover-style console cable, often USB to RJ-45. A crossover is for Ethernet links, not console access.","Objective 4.3",["A crossover cable connects Ethernet devices directly; it does not provide a serial console connection.","Coax jumpers are for cable TV and modems, not Cisco console ports.","Single-mode fiber carries data over long distances and has no console function.",null],2],["cs53",0,4,"A PC can reach other PCs on its subnet but nothing on other networks. Its IP and mask are correct. What is the most likely missing setting?",["DNS suffix","Default gateway","MAC address","Proxy server port number"],1,"The default gateway is where a host sends traffic for remote networks. A missing DNS setting would still allow pinging remote IP addresses.","Objective 4.4",["A missing DNS suffix affects name completion, but the PC could still reach remote networks by IP.",null,"Every network card already has a burned-in MAC address; it is not a setting the user configures.","A proxy setting affects only web traffic through a proxy and would not block all remote networks."],2],["cs54",0,4,"PC 10.0.5.10/24 sends a packet to 10.0.9.20. Where does the PC send the frame?",["Directly to 10.0.9.20's MAC address","To the DNS server","To every host as a broadcast","To its default gateway's MAC address"],3,"10.0.9.20 is outside 10.0.5.0/24, so the PC sends the frame to its gateway, which routes it. It cannot reach the remote host's MAC directly.","Objective 4.4",["The destination is on a different network, so the PC cannot ARP for or reach its MAC directly.","The DNS server resolves names; packets to an IP address are not sent through it.","Unicast traffic to a remote host is not broadcast; only the ARP request for the gateway might be.",null],3],["cs55",0,4,"What can a Layer 3 switch do that a Layer 2 switch cannot?",["Route traffic between VLANs or subnets","Learn MAC addresses","Forward frames within one VLAN","Provide Power over Ethernet to IP phones"],0,"A Layer 3 switch can route using IP addresses. Learning MACs and forwarding within a VLAN are basic Layer 2 functions that both perform.","Objective 4.4",[null,"Both Layer 2 and Layer 3 switches learn MAC addresses from source addresses.","Forwarding frames within a VLAN is a basic Layer 2 switch function.","Many Layer 2 switches also provide PoE; it is not tied to routing ability."],2],["cs56",0,4,"Which device separates networks and forwards packets between them using IP addresses?",["Hub","Unmanaged switch","Router","Access point"],2,"Routers connect different networks and make decisions by IP address. An unmanaged switch only forwards by MAC within one network.","Objective 4.4",["A hub repeats bits out every port and has no understanding of IP addresses.","An unmanaged switch forwards frames by MAC address within one network, not between networks.",null,"An access point bridges wireless clients onto the LAN; it does not route between networks by IP."],1],["cs57",0,4,"How does a switch learn which port a device is connected to?",["It asks the DNS server","It reads the source MAC address of frames arriving on each port","It reads the destination IP address of each packet","The administrator types every entry in manually"],1,"Switches record the source MAC and incoming port of each frame in the MAC address table. Destination IPs are used by routers, not for MAC learning.","Objective 4.5",["DNS maps names to IP addresses; switches never ask DNS about port locations.",null,"Layer 2 switches do not read IP addresses, and learning uses the source address, not the destination.","Static entries are possible but rarely used; switches learn entries automatically."],2],["cs58",0,4,"A switch receives a frame for a MAC address that is not in its MAC address table. What does it do?",["Drops the frame","Sends it back to the sender","Sends it only to the default gateway","Floods it out all ports in the VLAN except the one it arrived on"],3,"Unknown unicast frames are flooded within the VLAN so the destination can receive them. Dropping it would break new conversations.","Objective 4.5",["Dropping unknown unicast frames would break every new conversation before the MAC was learned.","A switch never sends a frame back out the port it came in on.","A Layer 2 switch has no concept of a default gateway for forwarding frames.",null],3],["cs59",0,4,"An office wants guest devices and staff PCs on the same switches but in separate broadcast domains. What should be configured?",["VLANs","A larger DHCP scope","Faster uplinks","A new SSID only"],0,"VLANs split one physical switch into separate broadcast domains. A larger DHCP scope only adds addresses, it does not separate traffic.","Objective 4.5",[null,"A larger DHCP scope only provides more addresses; all devices would still share one broadcast domain.","Faster uplinks add bandwidth but do not separate traffic.","An SSID alone does not separate wired traffic, and without VLANs guest and staff would still share one network."],2],["cs60",0,4,"Two PCs are on the same switch but in different VLANs. What is needed for them to communicate?",["A longer patch cable","A router or Layer 3 switch","A hub between them","The same MAC address on both"],1,"Each VLAN is its own network, so traffic between them must be routed. A hub cannot join VLANs.","Objective 4.5",["Cable length has no effect on VLAN separation.",null,"A hub operates at Layer 1 and cannot move traffic between VLANs.","Duplicate MAC addresses would cause problems and still would not join two VLANs."],2],["cs61",0,4,"A switch port connects to another switch and must carry traffic for VLANs 10, 20 and 30. How should the port be set?",["As an access port in VLAN 10","As a console port","As a trunk port","As a shutdown port"],2,"Trunk ports carry several VLANs using 802.1Q tags. An access port belongs to only one VLAN.","Objective 4.5",["An access port belongs to a single VLAN, so VLANs 20 and 30 could not cross the link.","A console port is for CLI management, not for carrying user traffic between switches.",null,"A shutdown port passes no traffic at all."],2],["cs62",0,4,"An engineer asks the technician to check which switch port a PC with a known MAC address is using. Which command should be run?",["show version","show mac address-table","show ip route","show running-config | include hostname"],1,"show mac address-table lists each learned MAC with its VLAN and port. show version shows software and hardware, not MAC locations.","Objective 4.5",["show version displays software version, uptime and hardware details, not MAC-to-port mappings.",null,"show ip route shows the routing table for IP networks, not where MAC addresses are connected.","This only shows the hostname line of the configuration, which has nothing to do with MAC locations."],2],["cs63",0,5,"A user reports that email stopped working. What is the first step of the troubleshooting methodology?",["Identify the problem by gathering information and asking questions","Replace the user's network cable and reboot the PC to see if it helps","Reboot the mail server","Document the fix in the ticket"],0,"Troubleshooting starts by identifying the problem: symptoms, scope and recent changes. Replacing hardware first skips the diagnosis.","Objective 5.1",[null,"Replacing hardware and rebooting are fixes attempted before the problem is understood, skipping diagnosis.","Rebooting the mail server is an untested fix that could disrupt every user.","Documentation is the last step, after the problem is fixed and verified."],2],["cs64",0,5,"A technician fixes a user's printer mapping. Which step should come before closing the ticket?",["Establish a theory of probable cause","Escalate to the network team","Verify full functionality and document the solution","Question the user about what changed"],2,"After a fix, verify it works for the user and document what was done. Establishing a theory happens earlier in the process.","Objective 5.1",["Establishing a theory is the second step, done before the fix, not after it.","Escalation is for problems you cannot solve; this one is already fixed.",null,"Questioning the user belongs to the first step, identifying the problem."],2],["cs65",0,5,"Two tickets arrive at once: one user cannot change a desktop wallpaper, and the whole sales floor has lost internet. How should they be handled?",["In the order they were received","Whichever is easier first","Wallpaper first because it is quick","Sales floor outage first because it affects more people and the business"],3,"Priority is based on impact and urgency; a department-wide outage outranks a single-user cosmetic issue. First-come order ignores impact.","Objective 5.1",["First-come order ignores impact and urgency, leaving a whole department down behind a cosmetic request.","Picking the easier job first ignores business impact.","A quick fix for one user's cosmetic issue should not delay restoring service to a whole department.",null],3],["cs66",0,5,"A help desk technician cannot fix a switch problem and escalates it. What makes the escalation most useful?",["A ticket with symptoms, affected users, tests run and results","A short message saying the network is broken","A phone call with no ticket","Asking the user to phone the network engineer directly and explain the problem"],0,"Clear notes on symptoms, scope and steps already tried stop the next tier repeating work. A vague message forces them to start over.","Objective 5.1",[null,"A vague message forces the next tier to start again from scratch.","A phone call with no ticket leaves no record for tracking or later review.","Pushing the user to explain it themselves loses technical detail and shifts help desk work onto the user."],3],["cs67",0,5,"A technician wants to capture traffic in Wireshark from a laptop's wired connection. What must be selected before starting?",["A display filter","The correct network interface","The file format .txt","The router's IP address"],1,"Wireshark captures on the interface you select, so pick the wired adapter. Display filters can be applied after capture.","Objective 5.2",["A display filter only hides packets on screen and can be applied after capturing; it does not decide what is captured.",null,"Wireshark saves captures as .pcapng or .pcap, and the file format is chosen when saving, not before capturing.","The router's IP address is not required to start a capture on a local interface."],2],["cs68",0,5,"After a capture, the technician wants to view only DNS traffic in Wireshark. What should be typed in the display filter bar?",["port 53 only","show dns","dns","filter=dns"],2,"The Wireshark display filter dns shows DNS packets. The other entries are not valid display filter syntax.","Objective 5.2",["port 53 only is not valid Wireshark syntax; the display filter form would be udp.port == 53 or tcp.port == 53.","show dns is not a Wireshark filter; it looks like a device CLI command.",null,"filter=dns is not valid display filter syntax; you type the protocol name itself."],2],["cs69",0,5,"An engineer asks for a packet capture to review later. How should the technician share it?",["Take a screenshot of the packet list","Copy the summary line into the ticket","Export it as a CSV of source IPs","Save it as a .pcapng file and attach it"],3,"Saving the capture as .pcapng keeps every packet so the engineer can open it in Wireshark. A screenshot loses the packet details.","Objective 5.2",["A screenshot shows only the visible summary lines and loses the full packet contents the engineer needs.","A single summary line leaves out every packet's headers and payload.","A CSV of source IPs drops ports, flags, timing and payloads, so the engineer cannot analyze the traffic.",null],2],["cs70",0,5,"ping 8.8.8.8 succeeds, but ping www.example.com says it could not find the host. Which area is most likely failing?",["Name resolution (DNS)","The physical cable","The default gateway setting","The Wi-Fi password"],0,"Reaching an IP address proves the path and gateway work; failing by name points to DNS. A cable or gateway fault would also break the IP ping.","Objective 5.3",[null,"A cable fault would break the ping to 8.8.8.8 as well, and that ping succeeded.","A missing or wrong gateway would stop the ping to 8.8.8.8, which is a remote address.","A wrong Wi-Fi password would prevent the connection entirely, so no ping would succeed."],2],["cs71",0,5,"A user cannot reach a website. tracert shows replies from the first three hops, then only asterisks. What does this suggest?",["The user's PC has no IP address","The problem, or an ICMP-blocking device, is beyond the third hop","The DNS server is down","The Wi-Fi password is wrong"],1,"The first hops answered, so the local link and gateway work; the issue or a firewall that drops traceroute probes lies further along the path. A PC with no IP would get no replies at all.","Objective 5.3",["A PC with no IP address would get no replies at all, but the first three hops answered.",null,"tracert to an IP runs without DNS, and a DNS outage would not make hops stop replying partway along the path.","A wrong Wi-Fi password would prevent any connection, so no hops would reply."],3],["cs72",0,5,"A server's website loads fine in a browser, but ping to the server times out. What is the most likely explanation?",["The server's network card is unplugged","The server's DNS record is missing","A firewall is blocking ICMP echo requests","The server is powered off"],2,"Firewalls often block ICMP while allowing web traffic, so a failed ping does not mean the service is down. An unplugged or powered-off server would not serve the page.","Objective 5.3",["An unplugged network card would stop the website from loading too.","A missing DNS record would stop the browser from finding the site by name, but the site loads fine.",null,"A powered-off server could not serve the web page."],3],["cs73",0,5,"Which command checks whether a DNS server can resolve a host name to an IP address?",["arp -a","ipconfig /release","netstat","nslookup"],3,"nslookup queries a DNS server directly. arp -a shows the local IP-to-MAC cache and does not test DNS.","Objective 5.3",["arp -a shows the local cache of IP-to-MAC mappings and does not query DNS.","ipconfig /release drops the PC's DHCP address; it does not test name resolution.","netstat lists network connections and listening ports, not DNS lookups.",null],1],["cs74",0,5,"A technician runs ping 127.0.0.1 on a PC and gets replies. What does this confirm?",["The PC's TCP/IP stack is working","The default gateway is reachable","The DNS server is responding","The internet connection is up"],0,"127.0.0.1 is the loopback address, so the reply shows only that the local IP stack works. It never leaves the PC, so it says nothing about the gateway.","Objective 5.3",[null,"Loopback traffic never leaves the PC, so it says nothing about reaching the gateway.","Pinging 127.0.0.1 does not contact the DNS server at all.","Loopback replies only prove the local stack works, not that the internet link is up."],3],["cs75",0,5,"An engineer needs to manage a switch remotely with an encrypted command-line session. Which protocol should be used?",["Telnet","SSH","RDP","TFTP"],1,"SSH provides an encrypted CLI session on TCP 22. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","Objective 5.4",["Telnet provides CLI access but sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.",null,"RDP is for graphical Windows desktops, not switch command-line management.","TFTP transfers files such as configs and images and is not an interactive session."],1],["cs76",0,5,"A technician needs to see a Windows server's desktop from home to change a setting. Which tool fits?",["Serial console cable with PuTTY","SSH","Remote Desktop Protocol","SNMP"],2,"RDP gives a remote graphical desktop on Windows. SSH provides command-line access, not the Windows desktop.","Objective 5.4",["A serial console cable needs to be physically attached to the device and gives a CLI, not a Windows desktop from home.","SSH gives a command-line session, not the graphical Windows desktop the technician needs.",null,"SNMP monitors and manages devices with polled values; it does not display a desktop."],2],["cs77",0,5,"A company's switches and access points are managed from a web dashboard hosted by the vendor, with no on-site controller. What is this approach called?",["Out-of-band console management","Telnet management","A local TFTP server","Cloud-based network management"],3,"Cloud-managed platforms such as Cisco Meraki let admins configure and monitor devices from a hosted dashboard. Console management requires a physical cable.","Objective 5.4",["Out-of-band console management uses a physical console cable or separate management network, not a vendor-hosted dashboard.","Telnet is an unencrypted command-line protocol, not a hosted web dashboard.","A local TFTP server stores configs and images on-site and offers no central dashboard.",null],2],["cs78",0,5,"A remote worker must reach internal file shares securely over the internet. What should they connect with first?",["A VPN","Telnet","FTP","An open guest Wi-Fi"],0,"A VPN creates an encrypted tunnel into the company network. FTP sends data unencrypted and does not provide network access.","Objective 5.4",[null,"Telnet is an unencrypted remote CLI protocol and does not give secure access to file shares.","FTP sends credentials and data unencrypted and does not provide access to the internal network.","Open guest Wi-Fi is unencrypted and does not connect the user to the company network."],2],["cs79",0,5,"Which Cisco command gives a short list of every interface with its IP address and up/down status?",["show running-config","show version","show ip interface brief","show cdp neighbors"],2,"show ip interface brief shows one line per interface with IP, status and protocol. show running-config shows the whole configuration and is far longer.","Objective 5.5",["show running-config prints the entire configuration, not a one-line-per-interface status summary.","show version shows software, uptime and hardware details, not per-interface IP and status.",null,"show cdp neighbors lists directly connected Cisco devices, not interface IP addresses and status."],1],["cs80",0,5,"An engineer asks which Cisco devices are directly connected to the switch and on which ports. Which command answers this?",["show ip route","show cdp neighbors","show inventory","show interfaces status"],1,"CDP discovers directly connected Cisco devices, showing their names, platforms and local ports. show ip route lists routes, not neighbors.","Objective 5.5",["show ip route lists known networks and next hops, not directly connected devices by port.",null,"show inventory lists the device's own hardware parts and serial numbers, not its neighbors.","show interfaces status shows each port's state, VLAN, duplex and speed but not which device is attached."],1],["cs81",0,5,"A technician is asked for the switch's IOS version and how long it has been running since the last reload. Which command shows both?",["show version","show running-config","show mac address-table","show ip interface brief"],0,"show version displays the IOS version, uptime, model and more. The running config does not show uptime.","Objective 5.5",[null,"show running-config shows the current configuration but not uptime.","show mac address-table shows learned MAC addresses and ports, not version or uptime.","show ip interface brief summarizes interfaces and IPs, not the IOS version or uptime."],1],["cs82",0,5,"The switch prompt shows Switch>. What must be typed to reach privileged EXEC mode to run more show commands?",["configure terminal","exit","show privilege","enable"],3,"enable moves from user EXEC (>) to privileged EXEC (#). configure terminal only works after you are already in privileged mode.","Objective 5.5",["configure terminal enters global configuration mode and only works from privileged EXEC, not from the > prompt.","exit leaves the current session or mode; it does not raise privilege.","show privilege displays the current privilege level but does not change it.",null],1],["cs83",0,5,"A technician forgets the exact show command syntax on a Cisco switch. What is the quickest built-in help?",["Reload the switch","Type show ? to list the options","Type help show all","Unplug the console cable"],1,"Typing ? lists the valid options at that point in the command, and Tab completes partial words. help show all is not IOS syntax.","Objective 5.5",["Reloading the switch interrupts the network and gives no help with command syntax.",null,"help show all is not valid IOS syntax; IOS uses ? for context-sensitive help.","Unplugging the console cable ends the session and gives no help."],2],["cs84",0,5,"show interfaces on a port shows a steadily increasing count of CRC errors. What does this most likely indicate?",["Wrong VLAN assignment","A DNS misconfiguration","A cabling or physical-layer problem","The port has no IP address"],2,"CRC errors mean frames arrive corrupted, usually from bad cables, connectors or interference. DNS and VLAN settings do not corrupt frames.","Objective 5.5",["A wrong VLAN stops traffic from reaching the right network but does not corrupt frames or cause CRC errors.","DNS problems affect name resolution above Layer 3 and cannot corrupt frames on a link.",null,"Switch ports normally have no IP address, and missing IP settings do not cause frame checksum errors."],3],["cs85",0,6,"A firewall rule list has no rule matching a new incoming connection. What happens to the traffic?",["It is allowed by default","It is logged and forwarded","It is sent to the DNS server","It is dropped by the implicit deny"],3,"Firewalls end with an implicit deny, so unmatched traffic is blocked. Allowing unmatched traffic would defeat the rule list.","Objective 6.1",["Firewalls default to blocking unmatched traffic; allowing it by default would defeat the rule list.","Unmatched traffic is dropped; it may be logged, but it is not forwarded.","Firewalls do not send unmatched traffic to the DNS server.",null],3],["cs86",0,6,"A small office firewall lets users browse the web, and reply traffic comes back in automatically without an inbound rule. What feature allows this?",["Stateful inspection","MAC filtering","Port mirroring","DHCP snooping on the LAN"],0,"A stateful firewall tracks outbound sessions and allows the matching return traffic. MAC filtering controls which devices can join, not return traffic.","Objective 6.1",[null,"MAC filtering controls which devices may connect; it does not track sessions to allow return traffic.","Port mirroring copies traffic to a monitoring port for analysis and does not permit traffic.","DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers on a switch; it does not manage return traffic through a firewall."],3],["cs87",0,6,"An administrator wants to stop anyone outside the network from using Telnet to reach internal devices. Which firewall rule fits?",["Permit TCP 23 from any","Deny TCP 23 from outside","Deny UDP 53 from outside","Permit TCP 443 from any"],1,"Telnet uses TCP 23, so denying it inbound blocks outside Telnet access. UDP 53 is DNS.","Objective 6.1",["Permitting TCP 23 from any would allow outside Telnet access, the opposite of the goal.",null,"UDP 53 is DNS; blocking it would break name resolution and would not stop Telnet.","TCP 443 is HTTPS; permitting it does nothing to block Telnet."],2],["cs88",0,6,"An attacker changes the amounts in a bank transfer while it is in transit. Which part of the CIA triad is affected?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Accountability"],2,"Unauthorized changes to data hurt integrity. Confidentiality is about data being read by the wrong people.","Objective 6.2",["Confidentiality is about data being seen by unauthorized people, but here the data was changed.","Availability is about systems and data being reachable when needed, not about data being altered.",null,"Accountability (tracing actions to users) is not part of the CIA triad and does not describe altered data."],2],["cs89",0,6,"A web server has an unpatched flaw that attackers could use. What is the unpatched flaw called?",["A security threat","A risk","An exploit","A vulnerability"],3,"A vulnerability is a weakness. The exploit is the code or method that uses it, and the threat is who or what might use it.","Objective 6.2",["A threat is the person or event that might take advantage of a weakness, not the weakness itself.","Risk is the likelihood and impact of a threat using a weakness, not the flaw itself.","An exploit is the code or technique that takes advantage of the flaw, not the flaw.",null],2],["cs90",0,6,"Thousands of infected computers flood a company's website with traffic until it stops responding. What type of attack is this?",["DDoS","Phishing","Man-in-the-middle","Tailgating"],0,"A distributed denial-of-service attack uses many systems to overwhelm a target and harms availability. Phishing tricks users with fake messages.","Objective 6.2",[null,"Phishing uses fake messages to trick users into giving up credentials, not traffic floods.","A man-in-the-middle attack intercepts or alters traffic between two parties rather than overwhelming a server.","Tailgating is a physical attack where someone follows an authorized person through a secure door."],1],["cs91",0,6,"An employee gets an email that looks like it is from IT, asking them to click a link and re-enter their password. What is this?",["Ransomware","Phishing","A DoS attack","A brute-force attack"],1,"Phishing uses fake messages to steal credentials or install malware. Brute force guesses passwords rather than asking for them.","Objective 6.2",["Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; this email is trying to collect a password.",null,"A DoS attack floods a system to make it unavailable, not to trick a user.","Brute force guesses passwords automatically instead of asking the user to type one in."],1],["cs92",0,6,"Malware encrypts a user's files and displays a demand for payment to unlock them. What type of malware is this?",["A keylogger","Adware","Ransomware","A rootkit"],2,"Ransomware encrypts data and demands payment. A keylogger records keystrokes but does not lock files.","Objective 6.2",["A keylogger secretly records keystrokes, but it does not encrypt files or demand payment.","Adware shows unwanted ads; it does not lock files for ransom.",null,"A rootkit hides deep in the system to keep hidden access, but it does not typically encrypt files for payment."],1],["cs93",0,6,"A customer is setting up a new home router. Which wireless security option should the technician recommend?",["WPA3-Personal","WEP","Open with a hidden SSID","WPA with TKIP"],0,"WPA3-Personal is the current, strongest option for homes. WEP and TKIP are broken, and hiding an SSID provides no real security.","Objective 6.3",[null,"WEP is broken and can be cracked in minutes, so it should never be used.","Open Wi-Fi sends traffic unencrypted, and a hidden SSID is easily discovered from client probes.","WPA with TKIP is deprecated and has known weaknesses, so it is not a recommended option."],1],["cs94",0,6,"A home router offers a push-button setting for connecting devices without typing the passphrase. Why should it usually be turned off?",["It forces clients to use 6 GHz","It lowers Wi-Fi range","It changes the SSID and channel every day automatically","WPS can be attacked to reveal the network key"],3,"Wi-Fi Protected Setup, especially the PIN method, has known weaknesses that can leak the passphrase. It has no effect on range or band.","Objective 6.3",["WPS has nothing to do with which band clients use.","WPS does not affect the radio's transmit power or range.","WPS does not rotate the SSID or channel; those remain whatever the router is set to.",null],3],["cs95",0,6,"What is the main difference between WPA2-Personal and WPA2-Enterprise?",["Personal uses AES and Enterprise uses WEP","Enterprise authenticates each user through 802.1X, Personal uses one shared passphrase","Personal works only on 5 GHz","Enterprise needs no passwords at all"],1,"Enterprise mode checks individual credentials against an authentication server with 802.1X, while Personal uses a pre-shared key. Both use AES, not WEP.","Objective 6.3",["Both modes normally use AES; WEP is a separate, obsolete protocol, not part of WPA2-Enterprise.",null,"WPA2-Personal works on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.","Enterprise uses per-user credentials such as usernames and passwords or certificates, so it is not password-free by design."],2],["cs96",0,6,"After enabling WPA3 on a home router, an older printer can no longer join. What is a reasonable fix that keeps security strong?",["Switch the router to WEP","Turn off Wi-Fi security completely","Use WPA2/WPA3 transition mode or connect the printer by cable","Share the router admin password with the printer"],2,"Transition mode lets older WPA2 devices join while newer ones use WPA3, and a cable avoids Wi-Fi entirely. WEP or open Wi-Fi would expose the whole network.","Objective 6.3",["WEP is broken and would expose every device on the network.","Turning security off lets anyone join and read traffic.",null,"The router admin password controls the router's settings; a printer does not use it and sharing it weakens security."],3],["x1",0,1,"During a VoIP call, voice packets arrive at uneven intervals, so the audio sounds choppy even though the average delay is low. What is this variation in delay called?",["Latency","Jitter","Bandwidth","Throughput"],1,"Jitter is the variation in delay between packets, and real-time traffic such as voice suffers when it is high. Latency is the delay itself, which the scenario says is low on average.","Domain 1: Latency, delay and jitter",["Latency is the delay itself; the problem here is that the delay keeps changing from packet to packet.",null,"Bandwidth is the capacity of the link, not how evenly packets arrive over time.","Throughput is the actual data rate achieved, which says nothing about uneven packet timing."],1],["x2",0,1,"A city government links its offices, libraries and fire stations spread across the whole city into one network. What type of network is this?",["CAN","PAN","WLAN","MAN"],3,"A metropolitan area network (MAN) spans a city or metro area. A campus area network (CAN) is smaller, joining buildings on a single campus.","Domain 1: Network types",["A CAN covers a single campus such as a university or business park, not a whole city.","A PAN covers a few meters around one person, such as Bluetooth devices.","A WLAN is a wireless local network in one site, not a network spanning a city.",null],1],["x3",0,1,"An online game sends player position updates many times per second, and a late update is useless because a newer one follows. Which transport protocol suits this best?",["UDP, because it does not wait to resend lost data","TCP, because it guarantees every update arrives in order","ICMP, because it is built to carry application data","TCP, because its handshake removes all network delay"],0,"UDP is connectionless and does not retransmit, so fresh updates keep flowing without waiting on lost ones. TCP's reliable, ordered delivery is tempting, but its retransmissions add delay to data that is already out of date.","Domain 1: TCP vs UDP",[null,"Guaranteed in-order delivery means waiting for retransmissions, which adds delay to data that is already stale.","ICMP carries control and error messages such as ping, not application data like game updates.","The TCP handshake adds a round trip before data flows; it does not remove delay."],3],["x4",0,1,"A technician is building a firewall rule to allow DHCP traffic between clients and a DHCP server. Which ports does DHCP use?",["TCP 20 and 21","UDP 161 and 162","UDP 67 and 68","UDP 53 and 123"],2,"DHCP servers listen on UDP 67 and clients use UDP 68. The FTP ports 20 and 21 are a common mix-up but belong to file transfer, not address leasing.","Domain 1: Common protocols and ports",["TCP 20 and 21 are the FTP data and control ports, not DHCP.","UDP 161 and 162 are used by SNMP polling and traps, not DHCP.",null,"UDP 53 is DNS and UDP 123 is NTP; neither is used for DHCP leases."],1],["x5",0,1,"A legacy file transfer application connects to its server on TCP port 21. Which protocol is it, and what is its main security weakness?",["SFTP, which cannot encrypt the file contents","TFTP, which requires overly complex passwords","FTP, which sends logins and data in clear text","HTTPS, which relies on an outdated, weak cipher"],2,"TCP 21 is the FTP control port, and FTP sends usernames, passwords and data unencrypted. SFTP is the secure replacement but uses SSH on port 22, not port 21.","Domain 1: Common protocols and ports",["SFTP runs over SSH on port 22 and encrypts both logins and file contents.","TFTP uses UDP 69 and has no authentication at all, so passwords are not its issue.",null,"HTTPS uses port 443 and is encrypted, so it does not match port 21 or this weakness."],2],["x6",0,1,"Which list shows the data units in order as data moves DOWN the stack from an application to the cable?",["Data, segment, packet, frame, bits","Bits, frame, packet, segment, data","Data, packet, segment, frame, bits","Segment, data, frame, packet, bits"],0,"Application data is placed in transport segments, then network-layer packets, then data link frames, and finally sent as bits. Swapping segment and packet is the most common error, but transport comes before network on the way down.","Domain 1: Encapsulation",[null,"This is the reverse order, which is what happens as data moves up the stack at the receiver.","Segments are created at the transport layer before the network layer builds packets, so these two are swapped.","Application data comes first and frames are built after packets, so this order is mixed up."],1],["x7",0,1,"A switch forwards traffic by looking up destination MAC addresses in its table. At which OSI layer does it mainly operate?",["Layer 1, physical","Layer 3, network","Layer 4, transport","Layer 2, data link"],3,"MAC addresses and frames belong to the data link layer, so a standard switch is a Layer 2 device. Layer 3 is tempting because some switches can route, but MAC table forwarding is Layer 2.","Domain 1: OSI model",["Layer 1 handles signals and cabling only; it does not read MAC addresses.","Layer 3 forwards packets using IP addresses, which is the job of a router.","Layer 4 deals with ports and segments, not MAC-based frame forwarding.",null],1],["x8",0,1,"A company moves its HR application from its own data center to a SaaS provider. Remote staff used to connect to the company VPN to use it. What is the MOST likely change for them?",["They must now use a console cable to reach the app","They can reach it over the internet without the VPN","They need a static public IP address on each laptop","They can only use the app from inside the office LAN"],1,"A SaaS app runs in the provider's cloud and is reached over the internet, so users no longer need a tunnel into the company data center. The office-only option is the reverse of what moving to SaaS does.","Domain 1: Cloud vs on-premises",["Console cables are for managing network devices locally, not for using business applications.",null,"SaaS apps are reached through normal internet access; laptops do not need static public addresses.","A SaaS app is hosted by the provider on the internet, so it is not limited to the office network."],2],["x9",0,1,"A developer wants to upload custom application code and have the provider run it, without managing servers or patching operating systems. Which cloud model fits?",["SaaS","IaaS","On-premises hosting","PaaS"],3,"Platform as a service provides a managed runtime where customers deploy their own code while the provider handles servers and OS. IaaS is tempting but leaves operating system management to the customer.","Domain 1: SaaS, PaaS and IaaS",["SaaS delivers a finished application to use, so the developer could not run their own custom code.","IaaS gives virtual machines whose operating systems the customer must manage and patch.","On-premises hosting means buying and running your own servers, the opposite of what is wanted.",null],2],["x10",0,1,"Which sequence correctly shows the TCP three-way handshake?",["SYN, ACK, FIN","SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK","ACK, SYN, SYN-ACK","SYN, SYN-ACK, FIN"],1,"The client sends SYN, the server replies with SYN-ACK, and the client answers with ACK to open the connection. Ending with FIN is tempting but FIN closes connections rather than opening them.","Domain 1: TCP three-way handshake",["FIN is used to close a connection, not to finish setting one up.",null,"The client starts with SYN, not ACK, so this order is wrong.","The third step acknowledges the server's SYN with ACK; sending FIN would start closing the connection."],1],["x11",0,1,"Administrators must transfer files securely to a server using SFTP. Which port must the firewall allow?",["TCP 21","UDP 69","TCP 22","TCP 23"],2,"SFTP runs inside SSH, which uses TCP port 22. Port 21 is tempting because of the similar name, but that is plain FTP.","Domain 1: Common protocols and ports",["TCP 21 is the control port for plain FTP, which is not encrypted.","UDP 69 is used by TFTP, which has no encryption or authentication.",null,"TCP 23 is Telnet, an unencrypted remote terminal protocol."],1],["x12",0,1,"Which situation is caused mainly by high latency rather than limited bandwidth?",["A satellite link waits a long time before each reply starts","A large download never goes above the plan's top advertised speed","Many users share a link and each gets a smaller share","A nightly backup job fills all of the link's capacity"],0,"Latency is the time it takes data to travel, and satellite links add a long delay before each response regardless of link speed. The shared-link option is tempting, but it describes capacity being divided, which is bandwidth.","Domain 1: Bandwidth vs latency",[null,"Hitting the plan's maximum speed is a bandwidth limit, not a delay problem.","Users splitting a fixed capacity is a bandwidth issue, not added delay.","A backup using all available capacity is about bandwidth being used up, not delay."],3],["x13",0,2,"A host is configured as 172.16.4.77/27. What is its usable host range?",["172.16.4.65 to 172.16.4.94","172.16.4.64 to 172.16.4.95","172.16.4.33 to 172.16.4.62","172.16.4.65 to 172.16.4.126"],0,"A /27 has blocks of 32 addresses, so .77 falls in the .64 block with broadcast .95, leaving .65 to .94 usable. Listing .64 to .95 is tempting but includes the network and broadcast addresses.","Domain 2: Network, broadcast and host range",[null,"172.16.4.64 is the network address and 172.16.4.95 is the broadcast address, so neither is usable.","That is the previous /27 block (network .32), which does not contain .77.","That range assumes a /26; a /27 block has only 32 addresses."],3],["x14",0,2,"Which host is on the same subnet as 192.168.10.100/26?",["192.168.10.130","192.168.10.60","192.168.10.120","192.168.10.200"],2,"A /26 splits the last octet into blocks of 64, so .100 sits in the .64 to .127 subnet along with .120. Address .130 looks close but is past the .127 broadcast address, so it is in the next subnet.","Domain 2: Same subnet check",["A /26 starting at .64 ends at .127, so .130 is in the next subnet.","192.168.10.60 is in the .0 to .63 subnet, not the .64 to .127 subnet.",null,"192.168.10.200 falls in the .192 to .255 subnet."],3],["x15",0,2,"Which IPv6 address does a host use to test its own network stack, like 127.0.0.1 in IPv4?",["fe80::1","::1","ff02::1","2001:db8::1"],1,"The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, the equivalent of 127.0.0.1. A link-local fe80 address is tempting because it is local, but it identifies an interface on the link, not the host itself.","Domain 2: IPv6 address types",["fe80::1 is a link-local address used on the local link, not the loopback.",null,"ff02::1 is the all-nodes multicast address, not the loopback.","2001:db8::/32 is reserved for documentation examples, not loopback."],1],["x16",0,2,"An internal IPv6 address begins with fd00. What type of address is it?",["Link-local","Global unicast","Multicast","Unique local"],3,"Unique local addresses fall within fc00::/7, and fd-prefixed addresses are the commonly used half; they are for private internal use. Link-local is tempting because it also starts with f, but it uses fe80::/10.","Domain 2: IPv6 address types",["Link-local addresses begin with fe80 and work only on one link.","Global unicast addresses come from 2000::/3, such as those starting with 2 or 3.","Multicast addresses begin with ff, not fd.",null],1],["x17",0,2,"While migrating, a company runs IPv4 and IPv6 at the same time on the same hosts and routers. What is this approach called?",["SLAAC","Dual stack","Port address translation","Link-local addressing"],1,"Dual stack means devices run both IPv4 and IPv6 at the same time, so either can be used. SLAAC is related to IPv6 but only describes how addresses are assigned.","Domain 2: IPv6 assignment and dual stack",["SLAAC is how hosts build their own IPv6 addresses, not running both protocols together.",null,"PAT lets many private IPv4 hosts share one public address; it does not run IPv6 alongside IPv4.","Link-local addressing gives each interface an address for its local link only."],1],["x18",0,2,"Which IPv6 address is written INCORRECTLY?",["2001:db8:0:0:1::5","fe80::1","2001:db8:a::1","2001:db8::5::1"],3,"The double colon may appear only once in an address, because using it twice makes the number of missing zero groups ambiguous. The address with 0:0 written out plus a :: is tempting, but it is valid since :: still appears only once.","Domain 2: IPv6 format",["This is valid: the double colon appears only once, standing for the missing zero hextets.","This is a valid link-local address with one double colon.","This is valid; leading zeros are dropped and :: is used just once.",null],2],["x19",0,2,"A PC shows the address 192.168.1.25, but a 'what is my IP' website shows 203.0.113.40. Why do they differ?",["The home router translates the private address to its public one","The PC has two network cards with different addresses","The website shows the DNS server's address instead","The ISP assigned the PC a public address automatically through APIPA"],0,"The router uses NAT/PAT to replace the PC's private source address with its own public address before sending traffic to the internet. The two-cards idea is tempting, but the website only ever sees the router's translated address.","Domain 2: NAT and PAT",[null,"The website sees the address traffic arrives from, which is the router's public address, not a second card.","'What is my IP' sites report the source address of the request, not the DNS server.","APIPA gives 169.254.x.x addresses when DHCP fails; it never assigns public addresses."],3],["x20",0,3,"What is a key difference between single-mode and multimode fiber?",["Multimode has a smaller core and reaches farther","Single-mode carries electrical signals, not light","Single-mode has a smaller core and longer reach","Multimode is used only for cable TV connections"],2,"Single-mode fiber has a very small core and usually a laser source, so it carries signals much farther than multimode. The reversed option is tempting but gets both the core size and the distance backward.","Domain 3: Fiber types",["This reverses the facts: multimode has the larger core and shorter reach.","Both fiber types carry light; neither uses electrical signals.",null,"Cable TV commonly uses coax; multimode fiber is mainly used for shorter data links."],1],["x21",0,3,"When fitting an RJ-45 plug, a technician untwisted several centimeters of each pair. The link now shows errors. What problem is this most likely causing?",["An APIPA address on the PC","A duplicate SSID on the network","Crosstalk between the wire pairs","The wrong VLAN on the switch port"],2,"Twisting the pairs cancels interference between them, so untwisting too much lets signals bleed across pairs as crosstalk. A wrong VLAN is tempting but would block traffic, not create errors on the link.","Domain 3: Interference on copper",["APIPA is an addressing result of DHCP failure, not a cabling fault from untwisted pairs.","SSIDs are Wi-Fi network names and have nothing to do with copper terminations.",null,"VLAN assignment is configuration and would not cause physical-layer errors."],2],["x22",0,3,"Two older switches that do not support auto-MDIX must be connected with copper cable. Which cable should be used?",["Crossover cable","Straight-through cable","Rollover console cable","RG-6 coaxial cable"],0,"Connecting like devices such as two switches needs a crossover cable when the ports cannot auto-detect with MDIX. Straight-through is tempting because modern gear handles it, but these switches lack auto-MDIX.","Domain 3: Straight-through vs crossover",[null,"Straight-through connects unlike devices, such as a PC to a switch; like devices without auto-MDIX need crossover.","A rollover cable is used for console access, not for data links between switches.","RG-6 coax is used for cable TV and modems and does not fit Ethernet switch ports."],2],["x23",0,3,"Which connector is typically used for analog telephone lines and DSL phone connections?",["RJ-45","LC","BNC","RJ-11"],3,"RJ-11 is the small connector used for phone lines and DSL. RJ-45 looks similar but is wider, with eight pins for Ethernet.","Domain 3: Connectors",["RJ-45 is the larger eight-pin connector used on Ethernet cables.","LC is a small fiber optic connector, not a telephone connector.","BNC is a bayonet coax connector used for older video and networking equipment.",null],1],["x24",0,3,"A new laptop and access point both support Wi-Fi 6E. Why might the user choose the 6 GHz band?",["Better range through walls than 2.4 GHz","More channels and less crowding","Support for every older Wi-Fi client","No need for any Wi-Fi security settings"],1,"The 6 GHz band adds many wide channels and only newer devices use it, so there is less congestion. Better range is tempting, but higher frequencies travel less far than 2.4 GHz.","Domain 3: Wi-Fi bands",["Higher frequencies such as 6 GHz have shorter range and weaker wall penetration than 2.4 GHz.",null,"Only Wi-Fi 6E and newer clients can use 6 GHz, so older devices cannot join it.","6 GHz networks require WPA3 or Enhanced Open protection; they are not exempt from security."],2],["x25",0,3,"A user in a far bedroom gets a weak 5 GHz signal but a usable 2.4 GHz signal from the same router. Why?",["5 GHz serves only one device at a time","2.4 GHz offers more non-overlapping channels than 5 GHz does","5 GHz signals are blocked by WPA3 settings","2.4 GHz travels farther and passes through walls better"],3,"Lower frequencies lose less strength with distance and obstacles, so 2.4 GHz reaches farther while 5 GHz is faster at shorter range. The channel option is tempting but backward, since 5 GHz has more non-overlapping channels.","Domain 3: Wi-Fi bands",["5 GHz serves many devices at once; the difference here is range, not a device limit.","2.4 GHz has fewer non-overlapping channels than 5 GHz, and channels do not explain range.","Security settings do not change how far a radio signal travels.",null],2],["x26",0,3,"A salesperson's laptop has no Wi-Fi at a client site, but their phone has 5G service. How can the laptop get online?",["Pair the laptop with the phone's Bluetooth earbuds","Turn on the phone's mobile hotspot and join it","Set the laptop to use an APIPA address","Change the laptop's DNS server to 8.8.8.8"],1,"A mobile hotspot shares the phone's cellular data connection with the laptop over Wi-Fi. Changing DNS is tempting when names fail, but the laptop has no network link to use.","Domain 3: Cellular, hotspots and tethering",["Earbuds are audio accessories and provide no internet connection to the laptop.",null,"APIPA only allows local link communication and gives no internet access.","DNS settings do not help when the laptop has no network connection at all."],1],["x27",0,3,"An older CCTV system uses coaxial cable with a push-and-twist bayonet connector. Which connector is this?",["F-type","RJ-11","BNC","LC"],2,"BNC connectors lock onto coaxial cable with a quarter-turn bayonet and are common in older video systems. F-type is also coax, but it is threaded, as used on cable modems.","Domain 3: Connectors",["An F-type connector screws on with threads rather than a bayonet twist lock.","RJ-11 is a small modular plug for telephone lines, not coax.",null,"LC is a fiber connector with a push-and-latch design."],1],["x28",0,3,"On a Mac, which Terminal command shows the IP addresses assigned to each network interface?",["ifconfig","ipconfig /all","show ip interface brief","tracert"],0,"macOS still uses ifconfig to display interface addresses. ipconfig /all is tempting because of the similar name, but it belongs to Windows.","Domain 3: Checking connectivity on endpoints",[null,"ipconfig /all is the Windows command and is not how macOS lists interfaces.","That is a Cisco IOS command, run on network devices, not on a Mac.","tracert is the Windows route-tracing command and does not list interface addresses."],1],["x29",0,3,"Warehouse users lose Wi-Fi near tall metal shelving far from the only access point, which runs on 2.4 GHz channel 6. What is the BEST fix?",["Add access points closer to those users","Move the access point to channel 3","Change security from WPA2 to WPA3","Increase the DHCP lease time on the laptops"],0,"Distance and metal obstructions weaken the signal, so more access points near the users give reliable coverage. Changing channels is tempting, but channel 3 overlaps its neighbors and does not fix a weak signal.","Domain 3: Interference and distance",[null,"Channel 3 overlaps channels 1 and 6 and does nothing about distance or metal blocking the signal.","The security protocol does not affect signal strength or coverage.","Lease time controls address renewal, not radio coverage."],3],["x30",0,4,"A new wireless access point is mounted on a ceiling where there is no power outlet. What lets it be powered through its network cable?",["An SFP fiber module","A console cable","A PoE-capable switch port","A trunk port setting"],2,"Power over Ethernet sends power over the same copper cable that carries data, which suits access points, phones and cameras. A trunk port is tempting because APs often need several VLANs, but trunking supplies no power.","Domain 4: Power over Ethernet",["SFP modules connect fiber or other media and do not deliver power to devices.","Console cables are for configuring devices, not supplying power.",null,"Trunking carries multiple VLANs; it does not power the connected device."],1],["x31",0,4,"A switch has learned that PC B is on port 5. PC A on port 3 sends a frame to PC B's MAC address. What does the switch do?",["Floods the frame out every port","Forwards the frame out port 5 only","Sends the frame to the router first","Drops the frame because PC B is already known"],1,"When the destination MAC is in the table, the switch forwards the frame only out the matching port. Flooding is tempting but applies only to unknown destinations.","Domain 4: MAC address tables",["Flooding happens only when the destination MAC is unknown, but here it is in the table.",null,"Traffic between hosts in the same VLAN goes directly through the switch, not via a router.","A known destination is exactly when the switch forwards the frame, not drops it."],2],["x32",0,4,"A hub is connected to switch port 2. The switch receives a frame on port 2 whose destination MAC it has also learned on port 2. What does the switch do?",["Floods it out all ports in the VLAN","Forwards it back out port 2","Sends it to the default gateway","Filters (drops) the frame"],3,"If the source and destination are on the same port, the switch filters (drops) the frame because the destination already saw it through the hub. Forwarding back out port 2 is tempting, but switches never send a frame out the port it came in on.","Domain 4: Forward, flood and filter",["The destination is known, so there is no reason to flood the frame.","The destination already received the frame through the hub, so the switch does not resend it on the same port.","Switches do not send local frames to a gateway; the gateway is a host setting.",null],3],["x33",0,4,"A PC sends an ARP request to the broadcast MAC address ffff.ffff.ffff. Where does the switch send it?",["Only the port connected to the router","All ports in that VLAN except the one it came in on","All ports in every VLAN on the switch","No ports at all, because switches always drop broadcast frames"],1,"A switch floods broadcast frames out every port in the same VLAN except the incoming one. Sending to every VLAN is tempting, but VLANs exist precisely to limit broadcasts.","Domain 4: VLANs and broadcast domains",["Broadcasts go to every host in the VLAN, not just the router's port.",null,"VLANs are separate broadcast domains, so a broadcast stays in its own VLAN.","Switches forward broadcasts within the VLAN; routers are what stop them."],2],["x34",0,4,"A switch port connects to a single desktop PC that belongs in the accounting VLAN. How should the port be configured?",["As a trunk carrying all VLANs","As a console management port","As a routed WAN interface","As an access port in the accounting VLAN"],3,"An end device needs an access port assigned to one VLAN. A trunk is tempting because it carries accounting too, but it is meant for links carrying several VLANs.","Domain 4: Access vs trunk ports",["Trunks are for links to switches, routers or APs that need many VLANs, not a single PC.","Console ports are dedicated hardware ports for device management, not an Ethernet port setting.","A routed WAN interface connects to other networks, not an end user's desktop.",null],1],["x35",0,4,"A router receives a packet for 172.16.40.5, but its routing table has no matching route and no default route. What happens to the packet?",["The router drops it","The router floods it out every interface","The router sends it back to the switch","The router forwards it to the DNS server"],0,"A router forwards only when it finds a matching route or a default route; otherwise it drops the packet and may send an ICMP unreachable message. Flooding is tempting, but that is what switches do with unknown MACs.","Domain 4: How a router decides where to send a packet",[null,"Routers do not flood packets; flooding is a switch behavior for unknown MAC addresses.","Returning the packet would not deliver it; with no route the router discards it.","DNS resolves names; it plays no part in forwarding packets that lack a route."],3],["x36",0,4,"Host 192.168.1.10/24 wants to reach 192.168.1.200. How does it send the frame?",["To the default gateway's MAC address, found by ARP","To the DNS server for a route","Directly to its MAC address, found by ARP","As a broadcast to every host"],2,"Both addresses share the 192.168.1.0/24 network, so the host uses ARP to find the destination's MAC and sends directly. Sending to the gateway is tempting, but that happens only for remote destinations.","Domain 4: Local vs remote networks",["The gateway is used only for remote networks, and 192.168.1.200 is on the same /24.","DNS resolves names; it does not provide routes or forward traffic.",null,"Only the ARP request is broadcast; the data frame goes to the one destination MAC."],3],["x37",0,4,"Why should both ends of every patch cable and every patch panel port be labeled?",["To increase the cable's bandwidth","Because unlabeled cables cannot carry PoE","To trace links fast and avoid mistakes","So the switch learns MAC addresses faster"],2,"Clear labels let technicians find the right cable fast during changes and outages, reducing mistakes. The bandwidth option is tempting only if labeling is confused with cable certification.","Domain 4: Cable management and labeling",["Labels do not change the electrical performance of a cable.","PoE works regardless of whether a cable is labeled.",null,"MAC learning comes from frame source addresses, not physical labels."],1],["x38",0,4,"An engineer asks you to connect a server to switch port Gi1/0/12 using the patch panel. Patch panel jack 12 is labeled for a printer. What should you do FIRST?",["Confirm which switch port each jack actually maps to","Plug the server into jack 12 anyway","Unplug the printer and reuse its cable","Use any free port and tell the engineer about it later on"],0,"Patch panel numbers do not always match switch port numbers, so verifying the mapping prevents outages and mispatching. Plugging in anyway is tempting because the numbers match, but the label shows they may not correspond.","Domain 4: Reading a network diagram",[null,"The label suggests jack 12 does not map to Gi1/0/12, so you could disconnect the printer or patch the wrong port.","This causes an outage for the printer without confirming the correct port.","The engineer planned a specific port, perhaps with VLAN settings, so a random port may not work."],3],["x39",0,4,"Which statement correctly compares a router with a switch that has no VLANs?",["The switch forwards between different subnets using a routing table","A router forwards using only its MAC table","The switch blocks broadcasts by default","A router separates broadcast domains; that switch does not"],3,"Each router interface is its own broadcast domain, while a switch without VLANs forwards broadcasts to all ports. Saying the switch blocks broadcasts is tempting, but switches flood them.","Domain 4: Routers vs switches",["A Layer 2 switch has no routing table and cannot route between subnets.","Routers make forwarding decisions using IP addresses and routing tables.","A switch floods broadcasts to all its ports; it does not block them.",null],2],["x40",0,4,"A frame crosses an 802.1Q trunk between two switches. How does the receiving switch know which VLAN it belongs to?",["The destination IP address shows the VLAN","A VLAN tag is added to the Ethernet frame","Each VLAN uses a separate physical cable","The switch checks the sender's DNS name"],1,"802.1Q inserts a tag holding the VLAN ID into frames on the trunk, apart from the untagged native VLAN. Separate cables are tempting, but the whole point of a trunk is to share one link.","Domain 4: VLANs and trunks",["Switches do not read IP addresses to decide VLAN membership on a trunk.",null,"A trunk carries many VLANs over one link, so separate cables are not used.","DNS names play no role in Layer 2 VLAN identification."],2],["x41",0,4,"A PC is set to 10.1.1.50/24 with default gateway 10.1.2.1. It can ping local hosts but nothing on other networks. What is wrong?",["The DNS server cannot be reached","The switch port is set as a trunk","The mask should be /16 to reach remote hosts","The gateway is not on the PC's own subnet"],3,"A default gateway must be on the host's own subnet (10.1.1.0/24 here), so 10.1.2.1 cannot be reached and remote traffic fails. Widening the mask is tempting, but it only hides the real misconfiguration.","Domain 4: Default gateway",["A DNS problem would not stop pings to remote IP addresses.","If the port were misconfigured, local pings would also likely fail.","A wider mask would make the PC treat remote hosts as local, which does not fix routing.",null],3],["x42",0,5,"In the troubleshooting methodology, what comes right after establishing a theory of probable cause?",["Document findings and lessons learned","Test the theory to determine the cause","Verify full system functionality","Identify the problem"],1,"After forming a theory, you test it to confirm or reject the cause before planning a fix. Verifying functionality is tempting, but it belongs after the fix is implemented.","Domain 5: Troubleshooting methodology",["Documentation is the final step, after the fix is verified.",null,"Verification comes after the fix is carried out, not before the theory is tested.","Identifying the problem is the first step and happens before any theory is formed."],1],["x43",0,5,"A technician wants Wireshark to record only traffic to and from host 10.0.0.5 so the capture file stays small during a long capture. What should be used?",["A display filter applied afterward","Saving the file in .pcapng format","A capture filter set before starting","Following the TCP stream"],2,"Capture filters decide which packets are recorded, so the file only contains the wanted traffic. A display filter is tempting because it uses a similar idea, but it only changes the view.","Domain 5: Wireshark",["Display filters only hide packets on screen; every packet is still captured and saved.","The file format does not reduce what is captured.",null,"Follow TCP stream shows one conversation after capture; it does not limit recording."],3],["x44",0,5,"On a Linux PC, which command lists each router hop on the path to a destination?",["traceroute","tracert","nslookup","ip route"],0,"Linux uses traceroute to show each hop toward a destination. tracert is tempting because it does the same thing, but it is the Windows command name.","Domain 5: ping, traceroute and ipconfig",[null,"tracert is the Windows version of the command, not the Linux one.","nslookup queries DNS for name resolution and does not show hops.","ip route shows the local routing table, not the hops along a path."],1],["x45",0,5,"A Windows PC received a wrong address from a rogue DHCP server that has now been removed. Which commands will get it a new lease from the real server?",["ipconfig /release, then ipconfig /renew","ipconfig /flushdns","ping -t 127.0.0.1","nslookup with the renew option"],0,"Releasing and then renewing drops the bad lease and requests a new one from whichever DHCP server answers. /flushdns is tempting because it is also an ipconfig option, but it only affects DNS cache.","Domain 5: ping, traceroute and ipconfig",[null,"This clears the DNS resolver cache but does not change the IP address lease.","A continuous loopback ping tests the local stack and does not touch the DHCP lease.","nslookup is a DNS query tool and has nothing to do with DHCP leases."],2],["x46",0,5,"A technician plugs a console cable into a new switch that has no configuration. What else is needed to reach its command line?",["A web browser pointed at the switch's IP address","A Remote Desktop client","A terminal emulator such as PuTTY on the right COM port","The switch's SSH host key"],2,"Console access uses a serial session through a terminal emulator, which works before any IP settings exist. SSH is tempting for CLI access, but it depends on an IP address the switch does not have yet.","Domain 5: Remote access and console",["A new switch has no IP address configured, so a browser cannot reach it.","RDP is for Windows desktops, not network device consoles.",null,"SSH needs IP connectivity and configuration, which a new switch does not have yet."],2],["x47",0,5,"An auditor captures network traffic and reads a switch administrator's password in plain text. Which change fixes this?",["Move Telnet to a different port","Replace Telnet with SSH for remote management","Use a longer password with Telnet","Replace Telnet with TFTP"],1,"SSH encrypts the whole management session, including credentials, whereas Telnet sends everything in clear text. A longer password is tempting, but it would still be readable in the capture.","Domain 5: SSH vs Telnet",["Changing the port does not encrypt the session, so passwords stay readable.",null,"A longer password is still sent in clear text over Telnet.","TFTP is a file transfer protocol with no encryption or login."],2],["x48",0,5,"show interfaces status lists a switch port as notconnect. What should the technician check first?",["The router's routing table","The DNS settings on the switch","The IOS version on the switch","The cable and the device at the far end"],3,"notconnect means the port sees no link, so a missing or bad cable or a powered-off device is the likely cause. Checking routing is tempting, but routing cannot matter until the physical link comes up.","Domain 5: Show commands",["Routing is a Layer 3 issue; notconnect means no physical link was detected.","DNS does not affect whether a port detects a physical link.","The IOS version does not normally stop a single port from linking.",null],2],["x49",0,5,"An engineer wants to know which next hop a router will use to reach 10.20.0.0/16. Which command shows this?",["show mac address-table","show ip route","show cdp neighbors","show inventory"],1,"show ip route displays the routing table, including each network and its next hop. CDP neighbors is tempting because it shows adjacent devices, but not which one is used for a destination.","Domain 5: Show commands",["The MAC address table maps MACs to switch ports, not networks to next hops.",null,"CDP lists directly connected Cisco devices, not routes.","show inventory lists hardware parts and serial numbers."],1],["x50",0,5,"The engineer needs the serial numbers of a switch's chassis and its installed SFP modules for a hardware return. Which command should be run?",["show ip interface brief","show running-config","show ip route","show inventory"],3,"show inventory lists the chassis and installed modules such as SFPs, with product IDs and serial numbers. show version is related but was not offered, and running-config holds only settings.","Domain 5: Show commands",["This shows interface IP addresses and status, not hardware serial numbers.","The running configuration holds settings, not module serial numbers.","The routing table has no hardware information.",null],2],["x51",0,5,"Which command displays the configuration currently active in a Cisco switch's RAM?",["show running-config","show startup-config","show version","show interfaces"],0,"The running configuration is the active one in RAM. The startup configuration is tempting, but it is the saved copy in NVRAM that may not match current settings.","Domain 5: Show commands",[null,"The startup configuration is stored in NVRAM and loads at boot; it may differ from what is running.","show version shows software, uptime and hardware details, not the full configuration.","show interfaces shows interface counters and status, not the whole configuration."],1],["x52",0,5,"A technician types sh ip int b on a Cisco switch and gets the same output as show ip interface brief. Why does this work?",["A default alias sets up this shortcut","Abbreviations work only in configuration mode","IOS accepts any unambiguous abbreviation","IOS ignores letters it cannot parse"],2,"Cisco IOS accepts shortened commands as long as each abbreviation matches only one keyword, and Tab can complete them. The alias idea is tempting, but no alias is needed for this.","Domain 5: Cisco IOS basics",["It works because of abbreviation rules, not because an alias has been configured.","Abbreviations work in EXEC modes as well, as this example shows.",null,"IOS rejects ambiguous or invalid input rather than silently ignoring it."],2],["x53",0,5,"A user reports that Wi-Fi is slow every afternoon, while nearby coworkers are fine. Which information is MOST useful to gather first?",["The user's password, so you can sign in as them and test it yourself","The ISP contract number","The device type, its location, when it started and what changed","A list of every switch in the company"],2,"Gathering details about the device, place, timing and recent changes helps narrow the cause of a problem affecting only one user. The ISP contract is tempting for internet slowness, but others on the same connection are fine.","Domain 5: Help desk practice",["Technicians should never ask for users' passwords, and it does not describe the problem.","Coworkers are unaffected, so the internet service is unlikely to be the cause.",null,"A full switch list does not describe this user's specific symptoms."],2],["x54",0,6,"A new IP camera is installed with the manufacturer's default admin password. What is the MOST important first step?",["Change the default password to a strong, unique one","Hide the camera's SSID","Turn off the camera's logging","Leave it, since the camera is indoors"],0,"Default passwords are widely known, so attackers can log in unless they are changed. Leaving it because it is indoors is tempting, but network attackers do not need physical access.","Domain 6: Changing default credentials",[null,"Hiding a network name does not protect the admin account and is easily bypassed.","Disabling logs reduces visibility and does nothing to secure access.","Default credentials are published online and can be used over the network wherever the camera is."],1],["x55",0,6,"Which login uses two DIFFERENT authentication factor types?",["A password and a PIN","Two different passwords","A security question and a password","A password plus an authenticator app code"],3,"A password is something you know and an authenticator app on a phone is something you have, so this is MFA. A password and PIN is tempting because it is two steps, but both are knowledge factors.","Domain 6: MFA",["A password and a PIN are both something you know, so this is one factor type.","Two passwords are still only something you know.","Both are knowledge factors, so this is not true multifactor authentication.",null],1],["x56",0,6,"A company laptop is often used on hotel and cafe Wi-Fi. Which firewall protects it in those places?",["The office network firewall","A host-based firewall on the laptop","A stateful firewall on the home router","The ISP's DNS server"],1,"A host-based firewall runs on the device itself, so it protects the laptop on any network. The office firewall is tempting, but the laptop is outside its reach when traveling.","Domain 6: Host-based vs network firewalls",["The office firewall only filters traffic passing through the office network, not a hotel network.",null,"A home router protects the home network, not the laptop when it is elsewhere.","A DNS server resolves names and does not filter connections to the laptop."],2],["x57",0,6,"A power outage takes a company's only web server offline for six hours. Which part of the CIA triad is affected?",["Confidentiality","Integrity","Non-repudiation","Availability"],3,"Availability means systems and data are accessible when needed, and an outage directly hurts it. Integrity is tempting since outages can risk data, but nothing here was changed.","Domain 6: CIA triad",["No data was exposed to unauthorized people, so confidentiality is not affected.","No data was altered, so integrity is not the issue.","Non-repudiation is about proving who performed an action, not about uptime.",null],1],["x58",0,6,"Security researchers publish code that takes advantage of a known flaw in a router model. What is that code called?",["A vulnerability","An exploit","A risk","A threat actor"],1,"An exploit is the code or technique that takes advantage of a vulnerability. The vulnerability is tempting because it is closely related, but it is the weakness, not the tool used against it.","Domain 6: Vulnerability, threat, exploit and risk",["The vulnerability is the flaw itself; the code that uses it is something else.",null,"Risk is the likelihood and impact of harm, not a piece of code.","A threat actor is a person or group, not code."],2],["x59",0,6,"A cafe offers a Wi-Fi network with no security enabled and no password. What is the main risk to customers?",["The network is limited to the 2.4 GHz band","Customers' devices cannot get an IP address","Wireless traffic is not encrypted, so others nearby can capture it","The cafe's DNS server will be unable to resolve names for customers' devices"],2,"With no Wi-Fi security, frames are sent over the air without encryption, so nearby attackers can capture unencrypted traffic. The DNS option is tempting because users may notice connection issues, but open Wi-Fi does not break name resolution.","Domain 6: Open networks",["Security settings have nothing to do with which band is used.","DHCP works normally on an open network.",null,"Wi-Fi security does not affect DNS name resolution."],2],["x60",0,6,"A caller claiming to be a traveling executive pressures the help desk to reset their password right now. What should the technician do?",["Verify the caller's identity using the approved process first","Reset it right away because the caller is senior","Email a new password to whichever personal address the caller provides","Read a temporary password over the phone"],0,"Social engineers use urgency and authority, so identity must be verified through the approved process before any reset. Emailing the password is tempting because it avoids saying it aloud, but the address comes from the unverified caller.","Domain 6: Social engineering",[null,"Urgency and authority are classic social engineering pressures, not proof of identity.","An attacker could provide their own email address and receive the password.","This hands credentials to an unverified caller who could be an attacker."],3]]);
